{"title":"The economics of sleep","link":"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/~/918625364/0/marginalrevolution~The-economics-of-sleep.html","date":1747722218000,"content":"<blockquote><p>Full-time, prime-age male workers in the top income quartile sleep around half an hour less per day than those in the lowest quartile.</p>\r\n<p>At the macro level, average sleep duration decreases as a country’s GDP increases.</p>\r\n<p>Higher-income individuals allocate more time to other leisure activities, such as social outings and internet usage, substituting sleep.</p></blockquote>\r\n<p>Here is <a href=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/~/t/0/0/marginalrevolution/~https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S1570677X25000292\" target=\"_blank\">the paper</a> by Cristián Jara, Francisca Pérez, and Rodrigo Wagner. Via the excellent <a href=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/~/t/0/0/marginalrevolution/~https://www.nationalaffairs.com/blog/detail/findings-a-daily-roundup/top-to-bottom\" target=\"_blank\">Kevin Lewis</a>.</p>\r\n<p>The post <a href=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/~/t/0/0/marginalrevolution/~https://marginalrevolution.com/marginalrevolution/2025/05/the-economics-of-sleep.html\">The economics of sleep</a> appeared first on <a href=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/~/t/0/0/marginalrevolution/~https://marginalrevolution.com\">Marginal REVOLUTION</a>.</p>\r\n<img height=\"1\" width=\"1\" src=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/~/i/918625364/0/marginalrevolution\" />\r\n<div><a href=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/_/28/918625364/marginalrevolution\"><img height=\"20\" src=\"https://assets.feedblitz.com/i/fblike20.png\" /></a> <a href=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/_/29/918625364/marginalrevolution,\"><img height=\"20\" src=\"https://assets.feedblitz.com/i/pinterest20.png\" /></a> <a href=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/_/24/918625364/marginalrevolution\"><img height=\"20\" src=\"https://assets.feedblitz.com/i/x.png\" /></a> <a href=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/_/19/918625364/marginalrevolutio